<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gascony</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gascony is a historic region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and the Atlantic. Its name derives from the Vascones, Basque-speaking people who extended their rule north of the Pyrenees in the late 6th century. A duchy from the 7th century, it passed with Aquitaine to the English crown in 1152 and was held by English kings until the French conquest of 1453.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gascony.jpg" alt="Gascony" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Larrousiney · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MapOfGascony.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gascony was a province of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that succeeded the Duchy of Gascony, dated 602 to 1453. The region is vaguely defined: it lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and extends north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adour">Adour</a> as its other main river. Most definitions put it east and south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>. Its historical capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auch">Auch</a>. Gascony was historically inhabited by Basque-related people, and the Gascon language, usually classified as a variety of Occitan, has been spoken there from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The name comes from the same root as the word Basque, through Vasconia, Wasconia and Gasconia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before Roman rule the inhabitants were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquitani">Aquitanians</a>, who spoke a language related to Basque. Julius Caesar's lieutenants conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a> in the 50s BCE. In 297 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> made the territory south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novempopulania">Novempopulania</a>, the land of the nine tribes, which corresponds mostly to modern Gascony. It was given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in 416 to 418 and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> after their victory in 507. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders devastated towns including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> and Dax in 842 to 844, until Duke William II Sánchez defeated them at Taller in 982. The duchy then splintered into counties and viscounties such as Fézensac, Armagnac, Astarac, Bigorre and Béarn.</p><p>The 1152 marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> gave Henry control of Aquitaine and Gascony. The duchy functioned as an overseas English possession, sometimes called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s first colony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> confiscated it in 1294, beginning the Gascon War, and Edward I's three expeditions of 1294 to 1298 failed to recover it before the Treaty of Paris in 1303. In 1337 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI of France</a> took Gascony back into his hands, which precipitated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. The English were finally defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-castillon">Battle of Castillon</a> on 17 July 1453, and Gascony remained French. From the 17th century its government was united with Guyenne, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, until 1792.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gascony is divided between the region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Landes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees">Pyrénées-Atlantiques</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gironde">Gironde</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lot-et-garonne">Lot-et-Garonne</a>, and the region of Occitanie, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gers">Gers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hautes-pyrenees">Hautes-Pyrénées</a>, south-western Tarn-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haute-garonne">Haute-Garonne</a>. Its towns include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auch">Auch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>, Dax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lourdes">Lourdes</a>, Mont-de-Marsan, Pau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarbes">Tarbes</a>. It was the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry III of Navarre</a>, who became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1594, and it is the land of d'Artagnan, who inspired the character in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a>'s The Three Musketeers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gascony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Great, who ruled from the 990s until 1030, made the dukes among the most respected princes of the kingdom, and his son Guy-Geoffrey, who reigned as William VIII, acquired the Duchy of <b>Gascony</b> in the 1050s and 1060s, extending ducal lordship from the Loire to the Pyrenees.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The bond also carried old dangers: in 1337 Philip VI of France confiscated <b>Gascony</b> from his vassal Edward III, who was also King of England, and the Hundred Years’ War began.</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings Louis IX of France and Henry III of England signed the Treaty of Paris, by which Henry gave up his claims to Normandy, Anjou, Maine and Poitou and held <b>Gascony</b> as a duchy, paying homage for it to the French king.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-4">4 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He went on to recover northern France and <b>Gascony</b> from the English.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-17">17 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Three months after the English defeat at Castillon, Bordeaux surrendered to the French, ending three centuries of English rule in <b>Gascony</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-19">19 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>England kept <b>Gascony</b>, and the term Angevin Empire was coined by modern historians.</blockquote><a href="/state/angevin-empire">Angevin Empire</a></li></ul></section>
